{
  "description": "std-agent-assistant — an in-app assistant that operates the running app for the signed-in user. Each section's floating button opens it in a side panel docked beside the page (std-panel-launcher; a side sheet on small screens), where the conversation shows every step the assistant takes live as it works; /assistant is the same conversation as a full page, in the app's own shell (pass `appName` and `navItems`); past conversations and what the assistant remembers open in place from its toolbar (Back returns to the chat), on either surface. One request flows down a chain of std agent atoms on the bus: the conversation (std-agent-session) SENDs → CONVERSE → std-agent-context bounds it → std-agent-memory adds what it recalls → std-agent-tool-loop answers it through `call-service llm call-tools`, firing only the inputs and reading only the entities its `tools` allow, as the signed-in user → ASSISTANT_REPLIED goes back to the conversation, to memory (remembered) and to std-agent-trace (the run record). By default the tools are the program's own declarations: every `-> external` input (its @description is the tool text) and a read over every persisted entity, so composing building blocks composes the assistant's abilities. Any section's page gets the assistant with two traits, `XAssistant = AgentSession.traits.AgentConversation` and a std-panel-launcher dock: the conversation speaks the ASSISTANT_* contract from any orbital, so the assistant needs no per-section wiring. Import the orbital once per app: `orbital Assistant = Agent.orbitals.AgentAssistantOrbital { config { instructions: \"…\" } }`. The conversation, memory and run records live in AgentRecordsOrbital; retarget them onto your own owner-scoped entities (`ownerId` a relation to your [identity] roster, with @read/@create) with `entities { AgentMessage: …, AgentMemory: …, AgentRun: …, AgentChat: … }`. The conversation is kept across pages and reloads (each person's most recently used conversation is the current one);",
